Ok, update-- after some consideration (and also a private suggestion from someone on here, thank you!) I started considering the use of a categorical covariate, and finding this <https://bcss.org.my/tut/bayes-with-jags-a-tutorial-for-wildlife-researchers/occupancy-modelling/occupancy-with-a-categorical-covariate/> tutorial, I see a way I could have a separate intercept for each forest cover category (low =1, medium = 2, high = 3) from one categorical covariate column, as well as include my continuous covariates in my model ("the categorical and continuous covariates" section is particularly helpful). However, with the added complexity of having a MSOM with DA, species-specific random effects and an interaction term between the categorical covariate and my continuous fragmentation covariate..I would very much appreciate insight as to whether I am doing this properly, and later, interpreting the coefficients properly. For instance, not sure if I'm supposed to multiply the b0 coefficient by the bforcov[cover[i]][k] * fragED[i]  coefficient (see attached equation) for my model as is done in the simpler model in the tutorial linked above with b0[fire[i]] coefficient for the categorical covariate with two levels, "burnt" and "unburnt" sites.

I attached my equation of how I attempted to follow the above tutorial to adapt it to my MSOM. I also attached my datasheet for reference with the column "cover" representing my categorical covariate for the different forst cover categories. I'm also now just including the one fragmentation metric, edge density (ED).

As always, insights are much appreciated. Thank you!

Jordan

