Hi Marc,

Thanks so much for your helpful reply! If nothing else I can say I am very thorough and persistent, ha..so I very much appreciate everyone's helpful tips and insights! :)

This definitely seems like a a great way to do this with a linear modeling approach, and I appreciate the specification and explanation of how this would work. I will say I'm quite committed to continuing with trying to specify these models with the MSOM bayesian framework. I may be misinterpreting some of what you mean towards the end of your message though, so I will look back through and see if I better understand re-reading it (particularly your second "(2)" point in the last paragraph).

I did actually set up a MSOM where I did something very similar as you suggested--three different categorical covariate categories with "low, medium and high" for each forest cover category, and either a 1 or 0 for each site depending on the level of forest cover at each site. I did have some convergence issues as I think the model was a bit overparameterized so I may be able to work that out a bit and get a better result with more iterations/burn-in.

But seeing the example from Mike Meredith I linked to above (https://bcss.org.my/tut/bayes-with-jags-a-tutorial-for-wildlife-researchers/occupancy-modelling/occupancy-with-a-categorical-covariate/) under "categorical and continuous covariates" it seems that is an option to do something very similar as what you suggest but within a MSOM, and just with separate intercepts for each category. If I can figure out how to alter my code to address the error I ran into when attempting to run it, I would be eager to see if this approach would work as it seems most in alignment with my ultimate goals for this model. If anyone has any specific insights on this I would be very appreciative!

Thanks again to you and all respondents for your very helpful comments and suggestions, I greatly appreciate it!

Jordan

