Dear Jordan,

your suspicions are right: I did not want to imply you should use function lm() in R!

Rather, I was trying to emphasize that the structure of your covariate model, i.e., the description of the covariates and the way in which they are combined to explain variability in some parameter in the MSOM, is exactly the same as what you MIGHT specify were you using function lm() for fitting some normal linear regression model with one explanatory variable that is categorical (i.e., a factor) and another that is continuous. The form of the linear model (which, again, is the way in which the covariates explain "things") is identical to what you would have in a normal linear regression model that you would fit by writing lm(y ~ A * x), where A is a factor with three levels and x is a continuous explanatory variable. Just that in this case, y is an observed response, while in your case the "response" is some parameter of the MSOM. Thus, inspecting the meanings of the parameters in such a model fit with lm() may be helpful also for your understanding of the analoguous parameters in the MSOM case.

Best regards  --- Marc

